\paragraph{Why minimum intervention works.}
The central finding is that restraint in downweighting outperforms aggressiveness.
ESS-only reweighting (PPL 17.737) performs worse than uniform (17.694), while the dual-constrained adaptive $\bstar$ achieves 17.651 by selecting the \emph{smallest} $b$ that meets the quality floor.
The binding statistics (\S\ref{sec:regime}) explain why: in the 38.2\% of batches where $\aeff$ determines $\bstar$, the selected exponent is 4.9$\times$ lower than the ESS-only upper bound, preventing unnecessary suppression of borderline samples ($q \approx 0.4$--$0.6$) that carry useful training signal.
This behavior is robust across contamination levels ($\aeff$-binding rate: 38.2\% at $\rho{=}0.4$, 33.4\% at $\rho{=}0.6$) and seeds (CV ${<}2\%$).

\paragraph{$\aeff$ as a proxy for true $\alpha$.}
$\aeff$ correlates with the true batch-level real fraction at Pearson $r \approx 0.70$ across $B \in \{4, 8, 16\}$, with a conservative bias of $-0.03$ (Appendix~\ref{app:alpha_validation}): the proxy slightly underestimates, biasing the method toward less intervention.

\paragraph{Where the advantage manifests.}
The advantage appears entirely at evaluation (PPL 17.65 vs.\ 17.69 on human-authored text); training loss curves overlap (${\sim}1.4\%$ difference), consistent with reshaping the effective distribution rather than changing the optimization trajectory.
However, part of this PPL gain may reflect distribution matching (training closer to the human-only evaluation set): downstream benchmarks (Appendix~\ref{app:downstream}) show 4 of 5 tasks with small negative $\Delta$ (sign-test $p \approx 0.19$, $N{=}5$), suggestive but not significant.

\paragraph{Why not a fixed $b$?}
The aggregate mean $\bstar \approx 1.4$ as a fixed exponent yields PPL 17.693, no better than uniform, because it masks extreme per-batch heterogeneity (std $\approx$ 1.9, range $[0, 20]$).
The grid optimum ($b{=}0.3$) requires sweeping nine candidates per setting; the adaptive method eliminates this search entirely.

