\paragraph{Scale verification.}
At 1.7B parameters (Qwen3-1.7B, $\rho{=}0.4$), adaptive $\bstar$ achieves PPL 13.27 vs.\ 13.50 for uniform ($\Delta{=}{-}0.23$), ${\sim}5.75\times$ the 0.6B gap.
A training asymmetry applies (adaptive resumed with preserved optimizer state; uniform re-initialized), but the 0.23 gap far exceeds typical optimizer effects, supporting the directional finding that benefits grow with scale.

\paragraph{Detector coverage.}
We evaluate one detector family (TF-IDF + logistic regression).
Whether score calibration matters as much as raw discrimination for reweighting remains a hypothesis; a systematic ablation across detector architectures and quality levels is needed to test it.

tool_args.new_string:
\paragraph{Detector coverage.}
An N-gram perplexity detector (AUC\,=\,0.73) reverses the adaptive advantage ($\Delta{=}{+}0.13$), while TF-IDF+LR (AUC\,=\,0.94) produces consistent gains.
This suggests a minimum detector quality threshold (AUC\,$\gtrsim$\,0.85) for effective reweighting, but only two detector families have been tested; characterizing this threshold across architectures remains open.

\paragraph{Detector quality requirements.}
An N-gram perplexity detector (AUC\,=\,0.73) reverses the advantage ($\Delta{=}{+}0.13$): noisy $q$-scores produce noisy $\aeff$ estimates, causing the method to select harmful $\bstar$ values.
TF-IDF+LR (AUC\,=\,0.94) succeeds because its calibrated scores ($\mathrm{ECE}{=}0.048$) yield reliable $\aeff$, enabling accurate minimum-intervention decisions.

